        "narrative": "<p>Takucmich Lake is in Ecological Lake Class 3, which consists of 72 lakes in northeast Minnesota that are deep and have steep shorelines, have irregular shoreline shapes, and have clear and soft (unmineralized) water.  Takucmich Lake is typical of these lakes except that it is deeper (150 ft) than the lake class average of 77 ft.Takucmich Lake was thermally stratified on 9/1/99 and retained good oxygen for lake trout (more than 5 ppm) to a depth of 110 ft, where the temperature was 39 F.  Inlets are from Tesoker and Trillium lakes and the outlet is to Lac La Croix.  Lake bottom substrates along the shoreline are mostly boulder and rock, with small amounts of sand, gravel, and muck.  Aquatic plants are sparse and grow to a depth of 20 ft.This fish population assessment consisted of four shallow gillnets set in depths of 21 ft or shallower (64 F or warmer) and nine deep gillnets set in depths of 30 ft or greater (47 F or colder).  Previous investigations consisted of shallow-to-deep gillnets set through the thermocline in 1966 and 1974, deep-only gillnets in 1979, and separate deep and shallow nets in 1986.The total catch of fish in the shallow gillnets in 1999 of 7 fish/net (1 lb/net) was lower than normal for this lake class and was similar in numbers, but lower in weight than previous investigations in this lake.  Shallow water fish populations in 1999 were dominated by rock bass.  Rock bass numbers in 1999 (6/net) were higher than normal for this lake class and were higher than previous catches.  No northern pike were captured in 1999, down from an average catch of 4/net in previous investigations.  White sucker numbers in 1999 (0.2/net) were down from an average catch of 4/net in previous investigations.The total catch of fish in the deep gillnets in 1999 of 18 fish/net (8 lb/net) was similar to previous catches of 15/net (8 lb/net) in 1979 and 23 fish/net (10 lb/net) in 1986.  Deep water fish populations in 1999 were dominated by cisco.Cisco numbers in 1999 (16/net) were similar to previous catches of 13/net in 1979 and 19/net in 1986.  Cisco sizes in 1999 averaged 9\"; the largest was 13.3\".Lake trout numbers in 1999 (0.9/net) were lower than previous catches of 2.0/net in 1979 and 2.6/net in 1986.  Lake trout sizes in 1999 averaged 19\"; the largest was 29.2\" (9.2 lb).  Lake trout ages appeared to range from five to twelve, although their otoliths (ear bones) were difficult to read.  Many of the lake trout had roundworms in their air bladders.  Low levels of PCB's and moderate levels of mercury were found in lake trout collected during this assessment.Burbot numbers in 1999 (1.3/net) were similar to previous catches of 0.6/net in 1979 and 1.4/net in 1986.  Burbot sizes in 1999 averaged 16\"; the largest was 21.5\".</p>",
